==Irish==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ga|sga|cen|t=besides; without}}, from {{inh|ga|cel-pro|*kina|t=on this side of}}; compare {{cog|wlm|amgen|am-'''gen'''|t=otherwise}}, {{cog|br|ken|t=otherwise}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|unstressed}} {{IPA|ga|/ɡən̪ˠ/}}
* {{a|stressed|Munster|Aran}} {{IPA|ga|/ɡɑn̪ˠ/}}
* {{a|stressed|Connemara|Mayo|Ulster}} {{IPA|ga|/ɡan̪ˠ/}}

===Preposition===
{{ga-prep|n|l-}}

# [[without]]
# [[not]] {{qualifier|in conjunction with a verbal noun}}

====Usage notes====
* In standardised Irish, triggers {{ga-mut-link|l}} of velar consonants (''c, g'') and bilabial consonants (''p, b, m'') on unmodified nouns, e.g. {{m|ga|gan [[pingin|phingin]]|t=without a penny}}. Does not trigger lenition on modified nouns, e.g. {{m|ga|gan pingin ina [[póca|phóca]]|t=without a penny in his pocket}}. In the meaning ‘not’, does not trigger lenition on either a verbal noun or on the direct object of the verbal noun, e.g. ''gan {{l|ga|ceannaigh|ceannach}}'' ‘not to buy’, ''gan {{l|ga|pingin}} a shaothrú'' ‘not to earn a penny’.

* Unlike most Irish prepositions, ''gan'' governs the nominative, not the dative, and it does not form prepositional pronouns: {{m|ga|gan [[an]] t-[[arán]]|t=without the bread}}, {{m|ga|gan [[mé]]|t=without me}}.

